138
00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:18.040
the book. Now, the paradox. Every scholar who has defined play

139
00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:20.940
has defined it as useless. Carl Diem

140
00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:24.460
called it purposeless activity for its own sake. The historian

141
00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:26.460
Johann Huizinga called it not serious.

142
00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.460
The French intellectual Roger K. Lois called it an occasion of

143
00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.940
pure waste, and then wasted another 200

144
00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.880
pages writing about it. The most exam-ready piece here is Stuart

145
00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.040
Brown's Seven Characteristics of Play.

146
00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.020
Brown founded the National Institute for Play. Learn all seven.

147
00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.800
One, it's voluntary, something you're not

148
00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:50.400
obliged to do. Two, it has inherent attraction, it's fun. Three,

149
00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.620
freedom from time, you lose track. Four,

150
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.240
diminished consciousness of self, you lose yourself in the

151
00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.720
moment. Five, improvisation,

152
00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:04.480
make-believe, and invention. Six, continuation desire, you never

153
00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.000
want to stop. And seven,

154
00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:10.060
it is apparently purposeless. The neuroscientist Melvin Conner

155
00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:12.100
called play a central paradox of

156
00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.840
evolutionary biology. Here is the paradox with numbers on both

157
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.320
sides. On the cost side, young

158
00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.300
mammals burn up to 15% of their calories playing, calories they

159
00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:23.940
could have spent growing, and play

160
00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:28.880
gets animals killed. The example to remember is Peru, 1988. The

161
00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:30.740
zoologist Robert Harcourt watched

162
00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:35.160
southern sea lions attack a colony of sea pups. Of the 26 pups

163
00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.520
killed, 22 were caught up playing in

164
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.860
shallow tidal pools and never noticed the attack until it was

165
00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.360
too late. On the payoff side, three

166
00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:47.440
studies. First, rats allowed to play freely for three and a half

167
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.200
days had much higher levels of

168
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.420
brain-derived neurotrophic factor, BDNF, a protein that

169
00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.160
stimulates nerve growth in the amygdala and

170
00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:59.940
the prefrontal cortex, the areas tied to emotion and decision

171
00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.540
-making. Rats isolated during the period

172
00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.940
when they play the most staid, socially clumsy well into

173
00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:08.920
adulthood. Second, the neuroscientist Sergio

174
00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:12.900
Pellis measured 15 mammal species and found that species with

175
00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.980
relatively large brains played much

176
00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:18.640
more than small-brained ones. Third, at the University of

177
00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.080
Illinois, 36 students, half of

178
00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:24.880
them varsity athletes, had to cross a simulated busy street on a

179
00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:27.000
treadmill surrounded by video screens.

180
00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.780
The athletes made significantly more crossings, and the study

181
00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:32.680
leader's line is the one to memorize.

182
00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.720
They didn't move faster, it looks like they thought faster. Now

183
00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.120
the dolphins. This is the section most

184
00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:43.080
likely to show up on a quiz. Fox visits Gulf World Marine Park

185
00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.040
in Panama City Beach, Florida,

186
00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:49.180
to meet Stan Kukcich, a psychologist who studies rough-toothed

187
00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:51.180
dolphins. Three things to remember.

188
00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:55.500
First, balls beat everything. In a controlled five-year study of

189
00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:56.960
16 captive dolphins,

190
00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:00.760
they were far more likely to start spontaneous play with balls

191
00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:02.320
than with any other object,

192
00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:06.400
than with bubbles, or even than with each other. Fox's line is

193
00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:07.960
that if play is brain food,

194
00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.600
ball play is a protein bar. Why balls? He calls them kinetically

195
00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:13.160
interesting,

196
00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.240
the most animate of inanimate objects. They bounce, roll, can be

197
00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.920
