202
00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:36.260
and within 10 minutes what began as a frenzy of free play has

203
00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:37.260
become a disciplined,

204
00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.240
competitive, interspecies ball game with an unspoken but

205
00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.160
mutually understood set of rules.

206
00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:47.900
That is sport in miniature. Third thing, Kukcich explains. The

207
00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:49.940
mechanism with a phrase from Piaget,

208
00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.420
moderately discrepant events. Dolphins, like children, learn by

209
00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.040
slightly modifying the rules

210
00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.440
of their play to make it incrementally more stimulating. Just

211
00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.060
hard enough. And one number,

212
00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:06.120
the encephalization quotient, EQ, is brain size relative to body

213
00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.640
size. Humans are at 7.0,

214
00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:14.100
dolphins around 4.5, and the great apes only 1.5 to 3. More

215
00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:16.820
brain, more play. Now Conor's categories,

216
00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:21.160
which the lecture slides use. There are two axes. By players and

217
00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.520
object, solitary locomotor rotational

218
00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.760
play, which is your body alone, spinning, running, climbing.

219
00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:31.040
Object play, which is a ball or a stick or a

220
00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.880
stone, and social play, which needs a partner, so chasing,

221
00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:37.500
wrestling, pretending. By character,

222
00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:42.140
serious play, master play, rough and tumble, and adult play. And

223
00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:43.880
the function, childhood play

224
00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:47.740
rehearses bodily skills. It rehearses biopsychological roles

225
00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.280
like fighting and motherhood, and it rehearses

226
00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:54.920
social order, using exaggeration, repetition, and restraint. The

227
00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:56.740
slides also give play a nickname

228
00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:00.600
worth remembering. Behavioral fat, a stored surplus that a

229
00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:02.900
species can afford, and a guard against

230
00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:07.140
hardship. For more facts from Fox that are quiz-sized. 1. The

231
00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.420
play deficit. Children today spend 50% less

232
00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:14.560
time playing outdoors than children did in the 1970s. Children

233
00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:18.260
aged 10 to 16 average just 12.6 minutes a day

234
00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:22.540
of vigorous physical activity. That's from a 2004 study funded

235
00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:24.340
by the National Institute of Child

236
00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:28.480
Health and Human Development. 2. The throwing hypothesis. The

237
00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.160
evolutionary biologist William

238
00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:34.020
Calvin, in a book called The Throwing Madonna, argues that

239
00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.140
accurate one-armed rock throwing may

240
00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:39.420
have driven the first lateralization of function to the left

241
00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:41.560
brain, which is the same hemisphere that

242
00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:45.020
handles the rapid muscle sequencing that language depends on.

243
00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:48.240
His argument for why throwing and not fire or

244
00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:52.080
tools. Throwing offers an immediate return on investment. You

245
00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:53.620
kill the rabbit today. It's

246
00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:57.100
speculative, but you should be able to state it. 3. Hunter

247
00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:59.800
-gatherers had leisure. In the 1960s,

248
